about why the shadow transport minister  thinks sam terry is with us now hello to  mr terry um  how do you feel about defying um the  leader why are you doing it  i'm not defying anybody i'm the shadow  secretary of state for transport i'm  here supporting 40 000 low paid  transport workers who've decided to go  on strike look this is something that's  important that people realize these  workers are not massively well paid  they're safety critical workers they're  people that work in ticket offices these  are people often paid 25 000 pounds or  less labor will always stand on the side  of workers who are making very difficult  decisions  last few weeks in my constituency our  food bank ran out of food because people  couldn't afford to donate to the food  bank we've got doctors talking about  going on strike nurses we've got  communication workers in the postal  service we've got lecturers all across  the country industrial disputes are  breaking out for a very simple reason  this government doesn't care about  ordinary working people in britain  instead they've had 10 years of  austerity and not even able to come  forward with serious offers in terms of  at least even matching the level of  inflation for people's pay rises across  the public and private sectors  yeah earlier this week he said do not go  on to picket lines and you did  if kirsten was in number 10 i wouldn't  be standing here today because it  wouldn't be an industrial dispute if i  was running the department for transport  we would have set the parameters for the  14 train operating companies for network  rail to make sure and for that matter  tfl to make sure that there was enough  money coming forward and enough serious  discussions taking place with the tssa  rmt and on saturday the azdef unions to  make sure that we didn't need to be an  industrial dispute this is very simple  you can't turn around to workers that  kept britain running during the pandemic  and say you know what you're going to  have to suck on it we're going to give  you less than a rate of inflation so  therefore you need to accept a below  inflation pay cut that's clearly not  acceptable there's not a single labour  mp thinks that's acceptable and i  believe that kyocera doesn't think  that's acceptable the key thing today is  to say under the labour government these  industrial disputes would not be  happening it's the government the tory  government that's agitating for these  things to happen we've got two tory  leadership contestants in a bidding war  to dismantle 200 years of workplace  rights that we've built up in this  country that's what you get in tory  britain you get inflation out of control  10 years of austerity below inflation  pay offers and dismantling our hard-won  freedoms and riots that would never  happen under a labour government and i  tell you what the sooner we have a  general election and the sooner kia  stammer and i'll be working every single  day to get him into number 10 is in  charge of this country british workers  will be far far better off you might not  because you might get sacked  this isn't about me and what happens to  me this is about the 40 000 people that  decided to lose a day's pay people who  have paid not very much money at all  that's the focus of today it's about  saying there's a red line that needs to  be drawn in the sand because the way the  government are treating workers across  britain is absolutely disgraceful you  know they rush through legislation just  a few weeks before the end of parliament  to be able to bring in strike breakers  look at their disgraceful actions over  piano look at the disgraceful way they  fail to support workers at british  airways people in britain have had  enough k and it's about time people  started standing shoulder to shoulder  with workers because they are the people  that will vote in the next labour  government  um the chairperson for the labour party  has just been on the telly and she says  it's up to the whips to decide what  happens to you next do you think you  might lose your job for insubordination  my focus today as it always is is making  sure that labour's message of what we  are going to do just answer my question  because i have like it i'll let you do  that for a good five or six minutes now  my question is are you prepared to get  sacked for insubordination  i've not been into normal to anyone i  support kia osama i think he's by far  the best person to be prime minister of  this country i believe he will be the  prime minister of this country my job  today is to support transport workers as  a shadow transport minister to support  the travelling public look kay we've  just had the hottest days on record in  british history the climate crisis is  totally out of control and yet we're  here on a dispute when actually our  public transport networks should be  pumped full of money so that they are  able to work and to function we can get  people onto trains off of planes out of  their cars and onto trains this isn't  just about this dispute here today this  is about the future of our country and  the future of our planet and i tell you  what under a labour government this  dispute wouldn't be happening kierstarma  would be very clear that we would have  fair pay officers for every worker with  a public or private sector across this  country and i believe that is why we  will win the next general election  okay um how much do you think the rail  workers should get paid as a percentage  increase  i think it depends depending on the  grade depends on the different job i'm  not in those negotiations but i tell you  what is not acceptable offering people  below inflation pay rises that is  clearly not acceptable because that's  saying to people you need to have a pay  cut instead of a pay rise that's clearly  not something that's going to help this  dispute get ended anytime soon okay so  you're suggesting above inflation pay  rises for rail workers i'm guessing for  all workers  i'm suggesting that every worker  deserves a pay rise that is in line with  inflation and at the moment the rmt and  tsa have been offered four percent this  year when inflation is running at 11 and  then at 2 on the following two years  that is a real terms pay cut no worker  should be offered a pay cut as a way  forward whilst having a sort of gun put  to their head in terms of losing their  jobs remember many ticket office staff  are facing actually losing their careers  entirely because they're being told that  ticket offices are not going to be part  of the future in the railways we'll tell  that to disabled or blind passengers  trying to get onto trains or trying to  buy a ticket just doesn't work for them  instead of attacking our public  transport network the government should  be supporting it  okay um so you're suggesting all public  sector workers should get at least 11  what's that going to do to inflation  i didn't say that okay at all  that's exactly what you said you said  they should get at least this nation and  then you said serious negotiations  around the table exactly what you said  serious negotiations around the table  there needs to be serious negotiations  across every dispute that's going on in  this country because the reality is it  is not acceptable for people to be in a  situation where even the food banks are  running out of food how do you explain  to a worker having to put over 100 pound  into a tank of fuel or paying 1500  pounds a month just to sort of heat and  have electricity in a two-bedroom house  you know this is totally out of control  the government needs to get a grip and  instead of waging war on workers what  they need to do is actually support  ordinary workers in britain so public  you did say 11 for uh public sector  workers that's exactly what you said uh  are you going back on that now  i'm saying very clearly that inflation  is out of control but putting forward  pay rises that are serious that actually  because you have to remember right a lot  of public sector and private sector  workers have not had pay rises for more  than a decade so actually even if you  were to match inflation now you still  wouldn't be catching up with where  workers were  if they'd had you know rises in line of  inflation over the last decade at what  point k do you think it's fair for  workers to stand up and say enough is  enough we can't feed our families we  can't pay our bills we can't put fuel in  the car to get to work they can't even  afford to probably buy the train tickets  to get to work that is not acceptable  and people have had enough

